stituting equation 1 into equation 2:\n\nFrom equation 1, NA = x + MC - a + y\n\nBut from equation 2, NA = CM + a - x - y\n\nTherefore, equating these two expressions for NA:\n\nx + MC - a + y = CM + a - x - y\n\nLet me simplify:\n\nLeft side: x + MC - a + y\n\nRight side: CM + a - x - y\n\nBring all terms to left side:\n\nx + MC - a + y - CM - a + x + y = 0\n\nSimplify:\n\n( x + x ) + ( MC - CM ) + ( -a - a ) + ( y + y ) = 0\n\n2x + (MC - CM) - 2a + 2y = 0\n\nHmm. So, 2x + 2y - 2a + (MC - CM) = 0\n\nBut MC - CM is a bit confusing. Wait, MC is the length of segment MC, which is same as CM. Wait, no, MC and CM are the same. Wait, so MC - CM is zero. Wait, that can't be. Wait, hold on, MC is the same as CM. So, in that case, MC - CM = 0. So the equation becomes:\n\n2x + 2y - 2a + 0 = 0 => 2x + 2y = 2a => x + y = a\n\nSo, from this, we get that x + y = a.\n\nInteresting. So, the sum of the lengths AM (which is x) and BN (which is y) is equal to a, which is the length of BC. Hmm.\n\nSo, from the first two equations, we derived that x + y = a.\n\nLet me note that down.\n\nSo, x + y = a. That's one equation.\n\nNow, let me think about how to relate MC and AN. Because in equations 1 and 2, we also have MC and AN. Let me see if we can express MC and AN in terms of other variables.\n\nWait, MC is the length from M to C. Since M is on AB, perhaps we can express MC using the coordinates or using the law of cosines? Alternatively, maybe using Stewart's theorem?\n\nAlternatively, maybe coordinate geometry would be a good approach here. Let me try to assign coordinates to the triangle.\n\nLet me place point A at (0, 0), point B at (c, 0), and point C somewhere in the plane. Let me denote coordinates:\n\nA: (0, 0)\n\nB: (c, 0)\n\nC: (d, e)\n\nThen, point M is on AB. Since AB is from (0,0) to (c, 0), so M can be at (x, 0), where 0 < x < c.\n\nSimilarly, point N is on BC. BC goes from (c, 0) to (d, e). Let me parametrize point N. Let me parameterize BC. Let me let BN = y, so NC = a 