ion 5: flipped by m5 \u2192 once (T)\n\nPosition 6: flipped by m5 and m6 \u2192 two flips (back to H)\n\nPosition 7: flipped by m6 \u2192 once (T)\n\nWait, so the final state would be:\n\n1: T (flipped once)\n\n2: H (flipped twice)\n\n3: T (flipped once)\n\n4: H (not flipped)\n\n5: T (flipped once)\n\n6: H (flipped twice)\n\n7: T (flipped once)\n\nSo the pattern would be T-H-T-H-T-H-T. Which is the desired alternating pattern starting with T. So this works. Therefore, with four moves: 1-2, 2-3, 5-6, 6-7. So four moves.\n\nBut wait, the problem states \"the minimum number of moves required to win the game\". So according to this, four moves. But let's check if this is indeed minimal.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there's a way to do it in fewer moves. Let's see.\n\nWait, each move affects two coins. To flip four coins (1,3,5,7), each needs to be flipped once. Since each move can flip two coins, but they are adjacent. So to flip coins 1,3,5,7, we need to have moves that affect these positions. Since positions 1 and 3 are separated by one coin, maybe overlapping moves can help.\n\nWait, for example, flipping 1-2 and then 2-3 flips coins 1,2,3. But combined effect on coins 1 and 3 is one flip each, and coin 2 is flipped twice (back to original). So that's how the previous solution worked for coins 1 and 3.\n\nSimilarly, flipping 5-6 and 6-7 affects coins 5,6,7. Flips coins 5 and 7 once each, and 6 twice. So combined, four moves to flip coins 1,3,5,7. That seems efficient.\n\nBut maybe there's another combination. Let's think.\n\nSuppose we try to flip 1-2, 3-4, 5-6, and 7-... wait, there is no 7-8. So we can't flip 7 alone. But flipping 6-7 would also flip coin 6. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, flipping 1-2, then 3-4, then 5-6, then 6-7. Let's see:\n\nMove 1-2: flips 1 and 2 \u2192 T,T,H,H,H,H,H\n\nMove 3-4: flips 3 and 4 \u2192 T,T,T,T,H,H,H\n\nMove 5-6: flips 5 and 6 \u2192 T,T,T,T,T,T,H\n\nMove 6-7: flips 6 and 7 \u2192 T,T,T,T,T,H,T\n\nBut this results in T,T,T,T,T,H,T. Which is not alternating. So that's not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, maybe flipping moves that overlap more.\n\nFor example, flipping 1-2, then 2-3, then 4-5, then 5-6, then 6-7. Let's see:\n\nMove 1-2: T,T,H,H,H,H,H\n\nMove 2-3: T,H,T,H,H,H,H\n\nMove 4-5: T,H,T,T,T,H,H\n\nMove 5-6: T,H,T,T,H,T,H\n\nMove 6-7: T,H,T,T,H,H,T\n\nResulting in T,H,T,T,H,H,T. Which is not alternating. So that's five moves and still not correct.\n\nHmm. Alternatively, what if we try a different sequence.\n\nSuppose we start with flipping 2-3, then 4-5, then 6-7. Let's see:\n\nMove 2-3: H,T,T,H,H,H,H\n\nMove 4-5: H,T,T,T,T,H,H\n\nMove 6-7: H,T,T,T,T,H,T\n\nResulting in H,T,T,T,T,H,T. Not alternating. Still adjacent Ts.\n\nAlternatively, flip 1-2, 3-4, 5-6:\n\nMove 1-2: T,T,H,H,H,H,H\n\nMove 3-4: T,T,T,T,H,H,H\n\nMove 5-6: T,T,T,T,T,T,H\n\nResulting in T,T,T,T,T,T,H. Not alternating.\n\nAlternatively, flip 1-2, 2-3, 4-5, 5-6, 6-7:\n\nThat's five moves. Let's simulate:\n\nMove 1-2: T,T,H,H,H,H,H\n\nMove 2-3: T,H,T,H,H,H,H\n\nMove 4-5: T,H,T,T,T,H,H\n\nMove 5-6: T,H,T,T,H,T,H\n\nMove 6-7: T,H,T,T,H,H,T\n\nAgain, same as before. Not helpful.\n\nWait, going back to the initial solution that required four moves: flipping 1-2, 2-3, 5-6, 6-7. That results in T-H-T-H-T-H-T. Let's verify again:\n\nStart: H-H-H-H-H-H-H\n\nAfter move 1-2: T-T-H-H-H-H-H\n\nAfter move 2-3: T-H-T-H-H-H-H\n\nAfter move 5-6: T-H-T-H-T-T-H\n\nAfter move 6-7: T-H-T-H-T-H-T\n\nYes, that's correct. So four moves. Is that the minimal?\n\nCan we do it in three moves?\n\nLet's try to see.\n\nSuppose we try three moves. Each move flips two adjacent coins. To flip four coins (1,3,5,7) each once. Each move can affect two coins. So total flips needed: four coins each once. Total flips: 4. But each move contributes two flips, so three moves would contribute six flips. So total flips: 6, which is even. But we need four flips (even), but distributed such that four coins are flipped once and others are flipped even. But let's see.\n\nAlternatively