rnatively, overlapping moves can cancel out flips on certain coins. For example, using move 1-2 and then 2-3 flips coin 1 once, coin 2 twice (back to H), and coin 3 once. That's how we flipped coins 1 and 3 with two moves. Similarly, for coins 5 and 7, using moves 5-6 and 6-7. So that's two moves for two coins each. So total four moves. If we can find a way to cover coins 1,3,5,7 with three moves, but each move affects two coins, maybe overlapping in a way that one move covers two target coins. But since the target coins are all odd positions, which are separated by one coin, it's impossible to cover two target coins with a single move, since each move is adjacent. For example, a move can only cover positions like 1-2 or 3-4, which include one target (1 or 3) and one non-target (2 or 4). Therefore, it's not possible for a single move to flip two target coins. Hence, each target coin requires at least one move that affects it. Since there are four target coins, and each move can affect one target coin (since they are all odd positions), you would need at least four moves. Therefore, four is the minimal number of moves.\n\nWait, but wait. Let's check this logic. Each target coin (1,3,5,7) needs to be flipped once. Each move can affect at most one target coin, because the moves are adjacent pairs: 1-2, 2-3, etc. Each adjacent pair includes one odd and one even position. So for example, move 1-2 affects coin 1 (target) and 2 (non-target). Move 3-4 affects coin 3 (target) and 4 (non-target). Similarly, move 5-6 affects coin 5 (target) and 6 (non-target). Move 7-8 doesn't exist. Therefore, to flip each target coin once, you need at least four moves: one for each target coin. However, in our previous solution, we only used four moves to flip all four target coins: moves 1-2 (affects 1), 2-3 (affects 3), 5-6 (affects 5), and 6-7 (affects 7). But each of these moves also affects a non-target coin. However, by overlapping moves, we were able to flip the non-target coins an even number of times, canceling their flips. So this suggests that four moves are necessary and sufficient.\n\nTherefore, the minimal number of moves is four.\n\nBut let's verify again by checking the equations. When we considered the system of equations for flipping coins 1,3,5,7, we found a solution with four moves: m1, m2, m5, m6. Each of these moves is used once. So four moves. Since each move must be used at least once for each target coin, and there's no overlap that allows covering two target coins with a single move, four is indeed the minimal number.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be four moves. But let's confirm with an example.\n\nStarting configuration: H H H H H H H\n\nMove 1: Flip 1-2 \u2192 T T H H H H H\n\nMove 2: Flip 2-3 \u2192 T H T H H H H\n\nMove 3: Flip 5-6 \u2192 T H T H T T H\n\nMove 4: Flip 6-7 \u2192 T H T H T H T\n\nFinal configuration: T H T H T H T\n\nWhich is the desired alternating pattern. Therefore, four moves are sufficient.\n\nIs there a way to do it in three moves? Let's assume someone claims three moves. Let's attempt:\n\nSuppose we do moves 1-2, 3-4, 5-6, and 7-... but wait, three moves. Let's try:\n\nMove 1: 1-2 \u2192 T T H H H H H\n\nMove 2: 3-4 \u2192 T T T T H H H\n\nMove 3: 5-6 \u2192 T T T T T T H\n\nResult: T T T T T T H. Not alternating.\n\nAnother try:\n\nMove 1: 2-3 \u2192 H T T H H H H\n\nMove 2: 4-5 \u2192 H T T T T H H\n\nMove 3: 6-7 \u2192 H T T T T H T\n\nResult: H T T T T H T. Still adjacent Ts.\n\nAnother attempt:\n\nMove 1: 1-2 \u2192 T T H H H H H\n\nMove 2: 2-3 \u2192 T H T H H H H\n\nMove 3: 4-5 \u2192 T H T T T H H\n\nResult: T H T T T H H. Adjacent Ts at positions 4-5.\n\nNot good.\n\nAnother idea: Use overlapping moves more cleverly.\n\nMove 1: 1-2 \u2192 T T H H H H H\n\nMove 2: 3-4 \u2192 T T T T H H H\n\nMove 3: 5-6 \u2192 T T T T T T H\n\nMove 4: 6-7 \u2192 T T T T T H T\n\nBut that's four moves again.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps flipping some moves multiple times. For example:\n\nMove 1: 1-2 \u2192 T T