ximum number of original trinomials that may not have roots.\n\n### Key Steps and Analysis:\n1. **Discriminant Condition for Roots**:\n   - A quadratic \\(x^2 + px + q\\) has no real roots if its discriminant \\(p^2 - 4q < 0\\).\n   - For each trinomial \\(x^2 + a_i x + b_i\\), we check \\(a_i^2 - 4b_i < 0\\).\n\n2. **Sum of Trinomials**:\n   - The sum of the nine trinomials is \\(9x^2 + (\\sum a_i)x + \\sum b_i\\), which must have at least one root. This requires its discriminant \\((\\sum a_i)^2 - 36(\\sum b_i) \\geq 0\\).\n\n3. **Arithmetic Progressions**:\n   - Let \\(a_i = a_1 + (i-1)d_a\\) and \\(b_i = b_1 + (i-1)d_b\\).\n   - The sums \\(\\sum a_i = 9(a_1 + 4d_a)\\) and \\(\\sum b_i = 9(b_1 + 4d_b)\\).\n\n4. **Critical Condition**:\n   - The discriminant condition simplifies to \\((a_1 + 4d_a)^2 \\geq 4(b_1 + 4d_b)\\). This implies the average terms \\((a_{\\text{avg}}, b_{\\text{avg}})\\) must lie on or below the parabola \\(b = a^2/4\\).\n\n5. **Maximizing Non-Root Trinomials**:\n   - Points \\((a_i, b_i)\\) lie on a line in the \\(a\\)-\\(b\\) plane. To maximize trinomials above the parabola \\(b = a^2/4\\), the line must intersect the parabola such that a maximal number of points lie above it.\n   - Through analysis and examples, it was determined that the optimal arrangement allows up to 4 trinomials to lie above the parabola while satisfying the average condition.\n\n### Conclusion:\nThe maximum number of original trinomials that may not have roots is **4**, achieved by positioning the arithmetic progression such that four terms lie above the parabola while the average term ensures the sum's discriminant is non-negative.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{4}", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo solve this problem, we need to analyze the given quadratic trinomials and take into account the fact that their coefficients form arithmetic progressions.\n\n1. **Define the Quadratic Polynomials:**\n   We have nine quadratic trinomials:\n   \\[\n   P_i(x) = x^2 + a_i x + b_i \\quad \\text{for} \\quad i = 1, 2, \\ldots, 9\n   \\]\n   where the sequences \\(a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_9\\) and \\(b_1, b_2, \\ldots, b_9\\) are arithmetic progressions.\n   \n2. **Sum of Polynomials:**\n   Let's consider the sum of all nine polynomials:\n   \\[\n   P(x) = \\sum_{i=1}^9 P_i(x) = \\sum_{i=1}^9 (x^2 + a_i x + b_i)\n   \\]\n   Since each \\(P_i(x)\\) contains an \\(x^2\\) term and there are nine \\(P_i(x)\\):\n   \\[\n   P(x) = 9x^2 + \\left(\\sum_{i=1}^9 a_i\\right)x + \\sum_{i=1}^9 b_i\n   \\]\n   \n3. **Arithmetic Properties and Symmetry:**\n   Because \\(a_i\\) and \\(b_i\\) are in arithmetic progression, they have symmetric properties. Specifically:\n   \\[\n   a_i + a_{10-i} = 2a_5 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b_i + b_{10-i} = 2b_5 \\quad \\text{for} \\quad i = 1, 2, \\ldots, 4\n   \\]\n   Thus, the sums:\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^9 a_i = 9a_5 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\sum_{i=1}^9 b_i = 9b_5\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the polynomial \\(P(x)\\) becomes:\n   \\[\n   P(x) = 9x^2 + 9a_5 x + 9b_5 = 9(x^2 + a_5 x + b_5)\n   \\]\n   So, \\(P(x) = 9P_5(x)\\).\n\n4. **Existence of Roots:**\n   According to the problem, the sum \\(P(x)\\) has at least one root. Hence, \\(P_5(x)\\) must have at least one root \\(x_0\\):\n   \\[\n   P_5(x_0) = x_0^2 + a_5 x_0 + b_5 = 0\n   \\]\n\n5. **Pairs of Polynomials:**\n   Notice that for any root \\(x_0\\):\n   \\[\n   P_i(x_0) + P_{10-i}(x_0) = 2P_5(x_0) = 0\n   \\]\n   This implies that for each pair \\((P_i, P_{10-i})\\) for \\(i = 1, 2, 3, 4\\), at least one of the trinomials \\(P_i(x_0)\\) or \\(P_{10-i}(x_0)\\) must have a root.\n   \n6. **Counting Polynomials Without Roots:**\n   Since there are four pairs and one middle polynomial \\(P_5\\), at least five trinomials must have a root. Consequently, the maximum number of trinomials that do not have any roots is:\n   \\[\n   9 - 5 = 4\n   \\]\n\n7. **Example Verification:**\n   Verifying that it is possible for exactly four trinomials not to have roots, we construct