 of the first k primes.\n\n2. By induction, the product of the first k primes is at least 2^k.\n\n3. Therefore, any n with k distinct prime factors is at least 2^k.\n\n4. Taking logarithm base 2 of both sides, we get log_2 n \u2265k.\n\n5. Converting to any logarithmic base, we have log n \u2265k log 2.\n\nHence, the inequality holds for all positive integers n.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{\\lg n \\geqslant k \\lg 2}", "deepseek_solution": "To prove that for any positive integer \\( n \\), the inequality \\(\\lg n \\geqslant k \\lg 2\\) holds, where \\( k \\) is the number of distinct prime factors of \\( n \\), we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Understanding the Inequality**:\n   - The inequality can be converted to base 2 logarithms. If we let \\(\\lg\\) denote the logarithm base 2, the inequality simplifies to \\(\\log_2 n \\geq k\\).\n   - This is equivalent to proving \\( n \\geq 2^k \\).\n\n2. **Base Case and Inductive Step**:\n   - **Base Case**: For \\( k = 1 \\), the smallest \\( n \\) is 2, which satisfies \\( 2 \\geq 2^1 \\).\n   - **Inductive Step**: Assume the product of the first \\( k \\) primes is at least \\( 2^k \\). For \\( k + 1 \\), the product of the first \\( k + 1 \\) primes is the product of the first \\( k \\) primes multiplied by the \\((k+1)\\)-th prime. By the inductive hypothesis, this product is at least \\( 2^k \\times p_{k+1} \\). Since \\( p_{k+1} \\geq 2 \\), the product is at least \\( 2^k \\times 2 = 2^{k+1} \\).\n\n3. **General Case**:\n   - For any positive integer \\( n \\) with \\( k \\) distinct prime factors, the smallest such \\( n \\) is the product of the first \\( k \\) primes. This product is shown to be at least \\( 2^k \\) by induction.\n   - Therefore, any \\( n \\) with \\( k \\) distinct prime factors is at least \\( 2^k \\).\n\n4. **Logarithmic Conversion**:\n   - Taking the base 2 logarithm of both sides of \\( n \\geq 2^k \\) gives \\(\\log_2 n \\geq k \\).\n   - Converting this to any logarithmic base (e.g., base 10), we have \\(\\lg n \\geq k \\lg 2 \\).\n\nThus, the inequality \\(\\lg n \\geqslant k \\lg 2\\) holds for any positive integer \\( n \\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\lg n \\geqslant k \\lg 2}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n\n1. Suppose $n$ is an integer greater than 1, and let $p_1, p_2, \\ldots, p_k$ be the distinct prime factors of $n$. We can express $n$ in terms of its prime factors as follows:\n\n\\[\nn = p_1^{l_1} p_2^{l_2} \\cdots p_k^{l_k}, \\quad \\text{where } l_i \\geq 1 \\text{ for } i = 1, 2, \\ldots, k\n\\]\n\n2. Since each $p_i$ is a prime number and hence $p_i \\geq 2$ for all $i=1,2,\\ldots, k$, we have:\n\n\\[\nn = p_1^{l_1} p_2^{l_2} \\cdots p_k^{l_k} \\geq 2^{l_1} \\cdot 2^{l_2} \\cdots 2^{l_k}\n\\]\n\n3. By combining the exponents, the above inequality simplifies to:\n\n\\[\nn \\geq 2^{l_1 + l_2 + \\cdots + l_k}\n\\]\n\n4. Taking the base 2 logarithm on both sides of the inequality:\n\n\\[\n\\log_2 n \\geq \\log_2 \\left(2^{l_1 + l_2 + \\cdots + l_k}\\right)\n\\]\n\n5. Using the property of logarithms $\\log_b(a^c) = c\\cdot \\log_b(a)$, we obtain:\n\n\\[\n\\log_2 n \\geq l_1 + l_2 + \\cdots + l_k\n\\]\n\n6. Since each $l_i \\geq 1$ for $i=1,2,\\ldots, k$, we have:\n\n\\[\nl_1 + l_2 + \\cdots + l_k \\geq k\n\\]\n\n7. Therefore, substituting this lower bound into our inequality, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\log_2 n \\geq k\n\\]\n\n8. Using the change of base formula $\\log_2 n = \\frac{\\log n}{\\log 2}$, we can rewrite this as:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\log n}{\\log 2} \\geq k\n\\]\n\n9. Multiplying both sides by $\\log 2$, we obtain:\n\n\\[\n\\log n \\geq k \\log 2\n\\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\log n \\geq k \\log 2}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let \\( a_{1}, a_{2}, \\ldots, a_{2000} \\) be real numbers in the interval \\([0,1]\\). Find the maximum possible value of\n\\[ \n\\sum_{1 \\leq i < j \\leq 2000}(j - i) \\left| a_{j} - a_{i} \\right| \n\\]", "deepseek_reasoning":