\nComparing this with the expression for \\( a_n \\):\n\n\\( a_n = (1 +\u221a2)^n (3 +2\u221a2)^n + (1 -\u221a2)^n (3 -2\u221a2)^n \\)\n\nSo, if we set \\( a_n = b_m \\), then:\n\n\\( (1 +\u221a2)^n (3 +2\u221a2)^n + (1 -\u221a2)^n (3 -2\u221a2)^n = (1 +\u221a2)(3 +2\u221a2)^m + (1 -\u221a2)(3 -2\u221a2)^m \\)\n\nThis looks complex, but maybe if we can find a relation between n and m such that exponents in the terms match. For example, if we can set \\( (1 +\u221a2)^n (3 +2\u221a2)^n = (1 +\u221a2)(3 +2\u221a2)^m \\), then similarly for the conjugate terms. However, this seems too restrictive, but perhaps there's a way.\n\nAlternatively, notice that \\( (3 +2\u221a2) \\) is related to \\( (1 +\u221a2)^2 \\). Let's check:\n\n\\( (1 +\u221a2)^2 =1 +2\u221a2 +2 =3 +2\u221a2 \\). Exactly!\n\nTherefore, \\( (3 +2\u221a2) = (1 +\u221a2)^2 \\), and similarly \\( (3 -2\u221a2) = (1 -\u221a2)^2 \\).\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite \\( a_n \\) as:\n\n\\( a_n = (1 +\u221a2)^n ( (1 +\u221a2)^{2n} ) + (1 -\u221a2)^n ( (1 -\u221a2)^{2n} ) \\)\n\n= \\( (1 +\u221a2)^{3n} + (1 -\u221a2)^{3n} \\)\n\nWait, let's verify:\n\nOriginal expression for \\( a_n \\):\n\n\\( a_n = (1 +\u221a2)^n (3 +2\u221a2)^n + (1 -\u221a2)^n (3 -2\u221a2)^n \\)\n\nBut since \\( (3 +2\u221a2) = (1 +\u221a2)^2 \\), so \\( (3 +2\u221a2)^n = (1 +\u221a2)^{2n} \\). Therefore:\n\n\\( a_n = (1 +\u221a2)^n (1 +\u221a2)^{2n} + (1 -\u221a2)^n (1 -\u221a2)^{2n} \\)\n\n= \\( (1 +\u221a2)^{3n} + (1 -\u221a2)^{3n} \\)\n\nSimilarly, \\( b_m = (1 +\u221a2)(3 +2\u221a2)^m + (1 -\u221a2)(3 -2\u221a2)^m \\)\n\nBut since \\( (3 +2\u221a2) = (1 +\u221a2)^2 \\), we can write:\n\n\\( b_m = (1 +\u221a2)(1 +\u221a2)^{2m} + (1 -\u221a2)(1 -\u221a2)^{2m} \\)\n\n= \\( (1 +\u221a2)^{2m +1} + (1 -\u221a2)^{2m +1} \\)\n\nTherefore, we can express both sequences in terms of powers of \\( (1 +\u221a2) \\) and \\( (1 -\u221a2) \\):\n\n\\( a_n = (1 +\u221a2)^{3n} + (1 -\u221a2)^{3n} \\)\n\n\\( b_m = (1 +\u221a2)^{2m +1} + (1 -\u221a2)^{2m +1} \