vious substitution. Let me see if I can solve for t in terms of y.\n\nAlternatively, rearrange the equation:\n\n16(t - y)y(4 - t)=t\u00b2(4 - y)^2\n\nHmm, this is a quartic equation in variables t and y. Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, set t = ky, where k is a rational number. Then t =ky, so x =ky - y= y(k -1). Then substitute into the equation.\n\nThen left side:16(y(k -1)) y (4 -ky)=16y\u00b2(k -1)(4 -ky)\n\nRight side:(ky)^2 (4 - y)^2\n\nTherefore, equation:16y\u00b2(k -1)(4 -ky)=k\u00b2 y\u00b2 (4 - y)^2\n\nCancel y\u00b2 from both sides (y \u22600):\n\n16(k -1)(4 -ky)=k\u00b2(4 - y)^2\n\nHmm, but we have both y and k here. If we set k as a parameter, then we can solve for y in terms of k. Let's try that.\n\nEquation:16(k -1)(4 -ky)=k\u00b2(4 - y)^2\n\nExpand right side:k\u00b2(16 -8y +y\u00b2)\n\nLeft side:16(k -1)(4 -ky)=16[4(k -1) -k(k -1)y]=64(k -1) -16k(k -1)y\n\nEquation:64(k -1) -16k(k -1)y =16k\u00b2 -8k\u00b2 y +k\u00b2 y\u00b2\n\nWait, no. Expand right side:\n\nk\u00b2(4 - y)^2 =k\u00b2(16 -8y +y\u00b2)=16k\u00b2 -8k\u00b2 y +k\u00b2 y\u00b2\n\nTherefore, equation is:\n\n64(k -1) -16k(k -1)y =16k\u00b2 -8k\u00b2 y +k\u00b2 y\u00b2\n\nBring all terms to left side:\n\n64(k -1) -16k(k -1)y -16k\u00b2 +8k\u00b2 y -k\u00b2 y\u00b2=0\n\nSimplify terms:\n\n64k -64 -16k(k -1)y -16k\u00b2 +8k\u00b2 y -k\u00b2 y\u00b2=0\n\nLet me collect like terms:\n\nConstants: -64\n\nTerms with k:64k\n\nTerms with k\u00b2: -16k\u00b2\n\nTerms with y: [-16k(k -1) +8k\u00b2] y= [-16k\u00b2 +16k +8k\u00b2] y= (-8k\u00b2 +16k)y\n\nTerms with y\u00b2: -k\u00b2 y\u00b2\n\nSo overall:\n\n-64 +64k -16k\u00b2 +(-8k\u00b2 +16k)y -k\u00b2 y\u00b2=0\n\nLet me rearrange:\n\n-k\u00b2 y\u00b2 +(-8k\u00b2 +16k)y +(-16k\u00b2 +64k -64)=0\n\nMultiply both sides by -1:\n\nk\u00b2 y\u00b2 + (8k\u00b2 -16k)y +16k\u00b2 -64k +64=0\n\nThis is a quadratic in y:\n\nA y\u00b2 + B y + C=0, where\n\nA=k\u00b2,\n\nB=8k\u00b2 -16k,\n\nC=16k\u00b2 -64k +64.\n\nLet me compute the discriminant D=B\u00b2 -4AC.\n\