/y). Let's see.\n\nLet me first try to rewrite the first equation in terms of sqrt(x/y). Let's denote t = sqrt(x/y). Then sqrt(x/y) = t, so sqrt(xy) = sqrt(x) * sqrt(y) = sqrt(x/y * y\u00b2) = sqrt(x/y) * y = t * y. Wait, let's check that:\n\nsqrt(xy) = sqrt(x) * sqrt(y) = sqrt(x/y * y\u00b2) = sqrt(x/y) * sqrt(y\u00b2) = sqrt(x/y) * y. But sqrt(x/y) is t, so sqrt(xy) = t * y. Therefore, in terms of t and y, sqrt(xy) = t * y.\n\nBut then, sqrt(x/y) is t. So, substituting into the first equation:\n\n2x - t * y - 4t + 2 = 0\n\nBut x can be expressed in terms of t and y. Since t = sqrt(x/y), then t\u00b2 = x/y, so x = t\u00b2 y. Therefore, substituting x = t\u00b2 y into the first equation:\n\n2(t\u00b2 y) - t y - 4t + 2 = 0\n\nSimplify:\n\n2t\u00b2 y - t y - 4t + 2 = 0\n\nHmm, this seems to be a linear equation in y, given t? Let me check.\n\nWait, 2t\u00b2 y - t y is (2t\u00b2 - t)y, so the equation becomes:\n\n(2t\u00b2 - t)y - 4t + 2 = 0\n\nSo solving for y:\n\ny = (4t - 2)/(2t\u00b2 - t)\n\nBut t must be positive since x and y are positive, so t > 0. Also, the denominator 2t\u00b2 - t must not be zero. Let's check when 2t\u00b2 - t = 0:\n\n2t\u00b2 - t = t(2t - 1) = 0 => t = 0 or t = 1/2. Since t > 0, we have t \u2260 1/2. So as long as t \u2260 1/2, we can express y in terms of t.\n\nSo y = (4t - 2)/(2t\u00b2 - t) = [2(2t - 1)] / [t(2t - 1)] = 2/t, provided that 2t - 1 \u2260 0. Wait, if 2t - 1 \u2260 0, then we can cancel out (2t - 1):\n\ny = 2/t. However, if 2t -1 = 0, i.e., t = 1/2, then the original expression would have denominator zero, which is invalid. So as long as t \u2260 1/2, then y = 2/t. Therefore, from this substitution, we have y = 2/t, given t \u2260 1/2.\n\nSo, if we substitute y = 2/t into x = t\u00b2 y, then x = t\u00b2*(2/t) = 2t.\n\nSo, x = 2t and y = 2/t.\n\nTherefore, through substitution in the first equation, we can express x and y in terms of t, where t = sqrt(x/y). So, this substitution reduces the first equation to x = 2t and y = 2/t.