ructing auxiliary circles is the way to go.\n\nAlternatively, another way to think about it: The tangent length from a point to a circle is equal to the distance from the point to the center squared minus the radius squared, all under a square root. Therefore, setting the tangent lengths equal to d\u2081 and d\u2082 gives us two equations:\n\n\u221a(PO\u2081\u00b2 - r\u2081\u00b2) = d\u2081\n\n\u221a(PO\u2082\u00b2 - r\u2082\u00b2) = d\u2082\n\nSquaring both sides:\n\nPO\u2081\u00b2 = d\u2081\u00b2 + r\u2081\u00b2\n\nPO\u2082\u00b2 = d\u2082\u00b2 + r\u2082\u00b2\n\nTherefore, the point P must lie on the intersection of two circles: one centered at O\u2081 with radius sqrt(d\u2081\u00b2 + r\u2081\u00b2), and another centered at O\u2082 with radius sqrt(d\u2082\u00b2 + r\u2082\u00b2). Therefore, this confirms the earlier approach.\n\nTherefore, the construction steps are as follows:\n\n1. For each circle, construct a new circle whose radius is the hypotenuse of a right triangle with legs equal to the given tangent length and the original circle's radius.\n\n2. The intersections of these new circles are the desired points.\n\nHence, the construction is feasible with compass and ruler, following these steps.", "deepseek_solution": "To construct a point \\( P \\) such that the tangents drawn from \\( P \\) to two given circles are equal to the given segments \\( d_1 \\) and \\( d_2 \\), follow these steps:\n\n1. **Construct Auxiliary Circles**:\n   - For the first circle with center \\( O_1 \\) and radius \\( r_1 \\):\n     - Build a right triangle with legs \\( d_1 \\) and \\( r_1 \\). The hypotenuse \\( R_1 = \\sqrt{d_1^2 + r_1^2} \\).\n     - Draw a circle \\( C_1 \\) centered at \\( O_1 \\) with radius \\( R_1 \\).\n   - For the second circle with center \\( O_2 \\) and radius \\( r_2 \\):\n     - Build a right triangle with legs \\( d_2 \\) and \\( r_2 \\). The hypotenuse \\( R_2 = \\sqrt{d_2^2 + r_2^2} \\).\n     - Draw a circle \\( C_2 \\) centered at \\( O_2 \\)